  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2)/2, drawn at 0.2 times the loss, steeper left-right (x). Each step moves by -learning rate × (slope + noise). In x, steps overshoot above rate 0.4 and grow without limit above 0.8.",
    "Noise per slope component has standard deviation 1.5 × sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch size b: 1.5 at b = 1, exactly 0 at 64. It is centred on the true slope, so a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate), drawn 2× their true length on one shared scale. Mini-batch step: the move the ball makes next. Full-data step: the move with no noise.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 8 (diverged). A ball outside the drawn square is shown at its rim; the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next values. Trails hold the start plus up to 40 steps. Five steps a second at speed 1."
  ]
}